  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nWe are currently getting some much needed moisture across the area \nwith some areas of showers and isolated thunderstorms. This is \ncourtesy of a shortwave moving toward the area. It is getting an a \nassist from a 95 knot jet streak that is approaching southwestern \nWyoming and will cross southern Wyoming this afternoon and evening, \nputting much of the area in the left front quadrant of it to enhance \nlift somewhat. As a result, almost all areas will have a chance of \nshowers today. There will also be some embedded thunderstorms. \nHowever, with cloud cover around the area, surface instability may \nbe limited. The main threat from any storm would be locally heavy \nrain, with precipitable water values generally running anywhere \nfrom 125 to 150 percent of normal. A mitigating factor will be \nthat there will be steering flow, so any storms will have decent\nmotion and not be able to anchor. With more cloud cover, \ntemperatures will also run around 5 to 10 degrees cooler than \nSunday. Coverage of showers and thunderstorms should decrease \nafter sunset, ending generally from northwest to southeast, \nalthough with the jet still in the area overnight, isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms may linger south of a Casper to Rock \nSprings line through the night. \n\nAnd, we could see some higher elevation snow from this. Any \naccumulation would likely be restricted to above 11000 feet \nthough. The highest elevations of the Wind River Range, like the\nGannett Peak, have a 1 in 2 chance of seeing 3 inches of new \nsnow. There should be little or no impact to travel though.\n\nWe should then enter a quieter period of weather as ridging builds \ninto the area from the west, pushing drier air into western and \ncentral Wyoming. There may be just enough leftover moisture for some \nisolated showers in southern Wyoming, but this looks like the \nexception rather than the rule. Following that, things look mainly \ndry through the week and even into the weekend. A large majority of \nthe guidance keeps the moisture associated with an upper level \nlow and the remnants of Hurricane Polo to the south of the area\nthrough the forecast period. A weak cold front will move into \nnorthern and central Wyoming Wednesday night. This front is \nmoisture starved though, so it will likely only bring some \nclouds and somewhat cooler temperatures and a gusty breeze, \nespecially to northern Wyoming. Another front may move through \nFriday night or Saturday, but again it has little moisture to \nwork with so any showers would be isolated with the only impacts\nbeing somewhat cooler temperatures and some gusty breezes. \nTemperatures will generally average above normal, with Thursday \nand Sunday looking like the coolest days behind the front. But \neven here, temperatures will only fall back to near normal \nlevels. So, once we get past the next 24 to 36 hours, things \nlook fairly uneventful as we head into October.",
